{"step_index":3772,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T21:04:32Z","thinking":"A substantial reduction in noise items sent to the language model is observed with a 60% threshold, along with zero missed true matches. The previous failure to match \"توب جنج ادفانس\" was due to the suffix \"ادفانس,\" similar to other suffixes which would now be considered.\n\n\nFurther investigation reveals that the 60% threshold, while effective, might still produce false positives like \"كونفاجران\" matching \"فياجرا.\" Strict brand identity can be enforced by a prompt instruction explicitly forbidding different brands or generics, the candidate search requiring the first two letters to match or a length difference less than 40%. Dosage form conflicts also need consideration to further filter matches.\n\n\nDosage form discrepancies and low brand similarity ratios are actively filtering potential matches. The current filters are excluding candidates when the requested and candidate forms differ, like tablets versus syrup or cream versus suppository. Further, matches are being excluded if the brand similarity falls below a specified threshold, even if the forms match. I see that  'برونكوفين' and 'بروفين' are being checked.\n\n\nThe updated prompt, emphasizing \"STRICT BRAND IDENTITY - NO ALTERNATIVES OR GENERICS,\" should ensure that different brand names like 'برونكوفين' versus 'بروفين', and 'سينوبريت' versus 'سينوبريل' are rejected as 'no_match'. The system should now correctly identify them as different drugs. This change tests the strict enforcement of brand identity.\n\n\nStrict dosage form and strength matching are now implemented. Incompatible forms (e.g., tablet vs. syrup) will result in a \"no_match\" status. Numeric strength discrepancies also trigger a \"no_match\" to prevent incorrect substitutions. The equivalence of 1000mg to 1gm is noted, and the system now checks for standard pack sizes.\n\n\n```json\n{\n  \"results\": [\n 